Answer: The outside class must not be allowed to directly call the constructor. The constructor is made private.

```java
private PrimeMinister() {
}
```

A private constructor cannot be accessed directly from another class. For example:

```java
class Assembly {
public static void main(String[] args) {
PrimeMinister pm = new PrimeMinister();
}
}
```

This results in a compilation error because the constructor is private.

Answer: The return type should be the type of the object reference being returned.

```java
private static PrimeMinister ref;
public static PrimeMinister getPrimeMinister() {
return ref;
}
```

Here, ref is of type PrimeMinister, so getPrimeMinister() also has the return type PrimeMinister.

Answer: The Singleton implementation follows these steps: Make the constructor private. Create a private static reference variable. Create a public static method that returns the object reference. Check whether the reference is null. If the reference is null, create the object. If the reference is not null, return the existing reference.

```java
class PrimeMinister {
private static PrimeMinister ref = null;
private PrimeMinister() {
}
public static PrimeMinister getPrimeMinister() {
if (ref == null) {
ref = new PrimeMinister();
}
return ref;
}
}
```

The object can be accessed from another class:

```java
class Assembly {
public static void main(String[] args) {
```

PrimeMinister modi =

```java
PrimeMinister.getPrimeMinister();
```

PrimeMinister up =

```java
PrimeMinister.getPrimeMinister();
System.out.println(modi);
System.out.println(up);
}
}
```

Only one object is created. Both modi and up refer to the same object.

Answer: Only one object is created. During the first call: ref == null is true, so the object is created. After that, ref contains the object reference. Every subsequent call finds that ref is not null and returns the existing reference.

```java
PrimeMinister p1 = PrimeMinister.getPrimeMinister();
PrimeMinister p2 = PrimeMinister.getPrimeMinister();
PrimeMinister p3 = PrimeMinister.getPrimeMinister();
```

All three references point to the same object.

Answer: Yes. One object can have multiple reference variables.

```java
PrimeMinister p1 = PrimeMinister.getPrimeMinister();
PrimeMinister p2 = PrimeMinister.getPrimeMinister();
```

Here, p1 and p2 are different reference variables, but both refer to the same object. Number of objects = 1 Number of references = 2 The number of references can increase without creating additional objects.

Answer: If the condition is not checked and the method creates a new object every time, every method call can create another object.

```java
public static PrimeMinister getPrimeMinister() {
ref = new PrimeMinister();
return ref;
}
```

If this method is called 10 times, 10 objects can be created. Therefore, the null check is required to restrict object creation to one.

Answer: If the constructor is accessible from outside the class, an external class can directly use new.

```java
PrimeMinister pm1 = new PrimeMinister();
PrimeMinister pm2 = new PrimeMinister();
```

This allows multiple objects to be created and breaks the Singleton requirement. Therefore, the constructor must be private.

Answer: A normal President class can be converted into a Singleton class by adding a private static reference, making the constructor private, and providing a public static method.

```java
class President {
private int number;
private String name;
private int term;
public void passBill() {
System.out.println(name + " passing the bills");
}
private static President ref = null;
private President() {
}
public static President getPresidentInstance() {
if (ref == null) {
ref = new President();
}
return ref;
}
}
```

The object is accessed using:

```java
President p = President.getPresidentInstance();
```

The outside class cannot directly use:

```java
new President();
```

