Answer: POJO stands for Plain Old Java Object. A POJO class is a simple Java class used to represent objects with properties and behaviors. A typical POJO can contain private variables, constructors, getters, setters, and methods representing behaviors.

```java
class Teacher {
private int id;
private String name;
private String subject;
public Teacher(int id, String name, String subject) {
this.id = id;
this.name = name;
this.subject = subject;
}
public int getId() {
return id;
}
public String getName() {
return name;
}
public String getSubject() {
return subject;
}
public void teach() {
System.out.println(name + " is teaching " + subject);
}
}
```

Answer: A POJO can contain private members, constructors, getters, setters, and required behaviors. Example:

```java
private int id;
private String name;
private String subject;
```

A behavior can be implemented as:

```java
public void teach() {
System.out.println(name + " is teaching " + subject);
}
```

Getters and setters provide controlled access to private members.

Answer: Yes. A POJO class can also be converted into a Singleton class. For example:

```java
private static Teacher ref = null;
```

The constructor can be private:

```java
private Teacher() {
}
```

A public static method can provide access:

```java
public static Teacher getTeacherInstance(
int id,
String name,
String subject) {
if (ref == null) {
if (id == 0 && name == null && subject == null) {
ref = new Teacher();
```

} else {

```java
ref = new Teacher(id, name, subject);
}
}
return ref;
}
```

The attributes and behaviors can then be accessed through the returned reference.

